{"kind":"assert","text":"Cutover (05d618e2), head af897c20: three test questions besides Q-B1 (1dd30c16). Since 2762a0f4:\n- dc8ff428 re-records the CLI listing fixture (T3(c), one of two) with the add-then-remove instrumentation that the note records.\n- a831fc27 does T3(b): verify.test.ts:358 now expects \"a retained foreign body does not match its exact fact\".\n- bea75efc names replay task scopes by reference. verify.test.ts went from 18 failures to 4.\n- 0d8b3663 is a production fix: operand.ts and retained-use.ts used TS parameter properties, which Node type stripping refuses, so the replay CLI would not load.\n- af897c20 updates forms-operand, which passes 7 of 7.\n\nQ-V1. verify.test.ts genesis-kind case (about line 344) and integrity-mode case (about line 406). Both rewrite I.0 input.kind to \"link\". The genesis digest now binds the input, so replay reports \"the original genesis bytes do not bind the source page's sequence\" instead of `genesis-kind:`. Integrity mode reports mismatch, not consistent. (a) Build a genuinely sealed history whose genesis has the wrong kind, so each case still tests the kind check. (b) Expect the binding failure, and drop the kind check's coverage at this point. I lean to (a). Otherwise nothing tests that replay refuses a genuine genesis of the wrong kind.\n\nQ-V2. verify.test.ts 18.46 and 18.52 (Weighing) expect consistent. They now give incomplete: \"the outcome judgment requires verified source evidence; anchored raw copies cannot enter Native uses\". That rule is in the reviewed components (23e0c92d). (a) Serve verified source histories, so the cases keep testing a consistent weighing. (b) Expect incomplete. I lean to (a), for the same coverage reason.\n\nQ-C1. scope-index-applications.test.ts:147 \"isolates malformed scope results\" damages the result by setting refs.scope to the membership reference, and expects malformed-installation. ScopeRef has no kind now, so the projector accepts the damage. (a) Drop the case, because the place fixes the kind. (b) Add a projector check that refs.scope differs from refs.membership and refs.destination. (c) Keep the case with a damage the new shape can still detect, such as a wrong genesis. I lean to (c): the case tests isolation of a malformed result, not the kind field, and (b) adds a rule that nobody asked for.\n\nProcess note: the lane once used a bare `git stash` / `git stash pop`, at once, to compare one file before and after an edit. It restored its own files. From now on it uses WIP commits. The lane edits source only while the NF-4 gate runs."}